# The Squire (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md): Cosmin, a cooper's boy from Vallaki, has run away to
# Argynvostholt to swear the dead Order's oath and clear his family's name: the chronicle lists his ancestor Ioan, the
# Order's last squire, as fled. The Order doesn't take the living, so he means to wait on the squires' graves until
# he's dead. Ioan didn't flee: he cut his fellow squires' headstones, then rode with the dragon's last words for the
# valley. A squire's vigil at the graves, against the men-at-arms still fighting the last battle, wins Cosmin his
# place. Reached from the Vallaki gate's news, Sir Godfrey's "Heard anything interesting?" and Cosmin on the graves.
# Every line here is unvoiced for now.

# --- Sir Godfrey's news (godfrey:menu jumps here) ---

~ godfrey_news
if quest.the_squire == done
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: Our squire's out at the gate. Living, would you believe. He talks to the men-at-arms as if they could hear him. I think some of them can.
-> argynvostholt/godfrey:menu
endif
if not quest.the_squire
quest the_squire rumored
endif
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: Interesting. Yes. There's a boy out on the squires' graves. Living. Came up the ridge a week ago and asked to swear the oath. Vladimir told him the Order doesn't take the living.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [sad]: So he said he'd wait until he wasn't. He sleeps out there on the stones. It's very cold. He means it, I think. I'd have meant it, at his age.
-> argynvostholt/godfrey:menu

~ godfrey_ioan
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: Ioan. Ioan. (He shuts his eyes, which doesn't seem to help him much.) A thin lad with big hands. He cut stone for his father before he came to us.
if flag.vladimir_last_words_known or flag.ioan_stone_read
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: Let them go home. That was the word. My lord said it in the courtyard with his last breath, and somebody had to carry it down to the valley, because the knights wouldn't. I sent him. I sent Ioan. On my horse.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: And when the house had fallen Vladimir wrote "fled" against his name, and I let him. I forgot. Four hundred years I let a boy be a coward in our book because I forgot.
set ioan_cleared
quest the_squire truth
-> vigil_offer
else
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: He went. That night. I'm sure of that and nothing else. Whether he ran or rode, I can't find it. It's in here somewhere. (He taps the side of his helmet.) Most things are.
endif
-> argynvostholt/godfrey:menu

~ vigil_offer
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: He wants the oath. Vladimir won't give the living the oath, and he's right, in his way. But the oath was never the first thing a squire swore. First was the vigil.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: A night at the squires' graves, standing, with the banner. If the squires stand up for him, the Order can't say no. Not even Vladimir. The men-at-arms will come out of the courtyard for the living, though. They always do. He'll need people standing with him.
-> argynvostholt/godfrey:menu

# --- Cosmin, on the squires' graves ---

~ cosmin
if quest.the_squire == gone_home
# He stays to pack until he's said goodbye (Wiki finding 82).
set cosmin_packed
Cosmin: (He's packing the horse blanket into a roll.) Home. Ioan's stone says what it says. It'll have to be enough.
-> END
endif
if quest.the_squire == done
Cosmin: Squire Cosmin, of the Order of the Silver Dragon. The first living one in four hundred years, Sir Godfrey says. I've written to my mother. She's written back. Most of it was underlined.
-> END
endif
if flag.squires_vigil_won
-> after_vigil
endif
# The Order went to its rest before he could swear (Wiki finding 26): nobody is left to give the oath or the truth.
if flag.order_fate == "rest"
-> order_rests
endif
if not flag.cosmin_met
set cosmin_met
if not quest.the_squire or quest.the_squire == rumored
quest the_squire met
endif
Narrator: A boy of fourteen or so sits wrapped in a horse blanket on the last of the squires' small graves, with a wooden sword across his knees and frost in his eyebrows. He gets up when he sees you, because a squire stands.
Cosmin: Cosmin. Of Vallaki. Cooper's son. I'm here for the oath. They won't give it to me, so I'm waiting. It doesn't take long, in this cold, they say.
endif
* Why do you want it so badly? -> why
* [Persuasion DC 17] Come home. Your mother's at the north gate every night. -> home | stays
* [if not flag.ioan_stone_read] [Investigation DC 13] (Look at the last headstone, the one he's sitting on.) -> stone | stone_missed
* [if night and quest.the_squire == truth] (Stand the vigil with him.) -> vigil
* [if quest.the_squire == truth and not flag.hearing_held] Ask the Order's table to hear Ioan's case, here at the graves. -> hearing_intro
* Goodbye. -> END

~ order_rests
Cosmin: They've gone. All of them, the night the light came on in the tower. The squires too. There's nobody left to give the oath.
if flag.ioan_stone_read
Cosmin: But Ioan didn't run. His stone says so, and his stone was here before any of them. That'll have to be enough. I'm going home.
quest the_squire gone_home
-> END
endif
* [Investigation DC 13] (Look at the last headstone, the one he's sitting on.) -> stone | stone_missed
* Goodbye. -> END

~ why
Cosmin: My grandfather's grandfather's grandfather's, something, was Ioan, the Order's last squire. The chronicle up there says "Ioan: fled." That's what my family's been for four hundred years. The ones who fled.
Cosmin: If I swear the oath, there's a Vallaki boy in the book who didn't run. One will do.
-> cosmin

~ home
Cosmin: (He looks south, toward Vallaki, for a long time.) She's at the gate. Every night. (He sits back down on the grave.) Not yet. When his name's clean. Not before.
-> cosmin

~ stays
Cosmin: No. I'm sorry. I'm not going home a coward's grandson. I'll go home a squire or I won't.
-> cosmin

~ stone
set ioan_stone_read
Narrator: The last stone is cut more carefully than any of the others. On its back, low down where the frost doesn't reach, small letters, chiselled in a hurry by someone who knew how:
Narrator: I cut these for my brothers. I ride tonight with my lord's last word for the valley. Forgive me the haste. Ioan.
Cosmin: He didn't run. (He reads it again with his finger.) He didn't run. He was sent. Who sent him?
if flag.order_fate != "rest"
interject tag:curious: Somebody up at the house will know. Godfrey was there.
endif
-> cosmin

~ stone_missed
Narrator: A small stone with a name on it, like the rest, and frost in the letters.
-> cosmin

~ vigil
Narrator: Cosmin plants an old banner of the Order, a silver dragon on black gone grey, in the frozen ground at the head of the graves, and stands beside it with his wooden sword. The cold comes down off the ridge.
Narrator: At the darkest hour the small graves give up their squires: boys, most of them, grey and quiet, standing at the foot of their stones in a row, watching. And out of the courtyard, as Godfrey said, come the men-at-arms, still fighting the last battle, turning toward the only living things on the ridge.
Cosmin [afraid]: Stand. Stand. That's all it is. Standing.
combat squires_vigil

~ after_vigil
quest the_squire done
Narrator: When the last man-at-arms goes down into the frost, the squires come forward from their stones, one by one, and each of them touches the banner and goes back.
Narrator: Sir Godfrey comes down from the house with the Order's book under his arm, and a shield, and a pen that's been dry for centuries, and he writes for a long time.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: Ioan: rode with my lord's last word, on my horse, on my order. And under it, Cosmin: stood the vigil. Squire. (He blows on the page.) Vladimir can argue with the squires if he likes. I wouldn't.
-> squire_gift

# The shield is given in one place for both endings (a lasting item is placed once: tests/unit/test_loot_distinct.gd).
~ squire_gift
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: This was Ioan's. He left it at the gate the night he rode, because a shield slows a horse. It's caught every arrow that's come at the Order's gate for four hundred years. Take it. Our squire has a wooden sword and the right idea, and that'll do him for now.
give arrow_catching_shield
gold +150
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3 thistle +1 liriel_dawnsong +2 wren_featherfoot +1 ratatoille +1: You stood by Cosmin and cleared Ioan's name
-> END

# --- Without a fight: the Order's table hears Ioan's case (NPC plan batch 5; docs/plans/npc_depth_and_quests.md) ---
# Cosmin asks the table to hear his ancestor at the graves, the oldest form of petition. Evidence in Ioan's favour: the words on
# the back of the headstone, the dragon's last words, Godfrey's own memory, History (the Order's rolls) and Persuasion. Three
# votes carry it; short of that the table isn't moved, and the vigil above is still open.

~ hearing_intro
set hearing_held
set hearing_votes = 0
Narrator: Five dead knights take their places among the small graves, seating themselves on the frost as if a long oak table still stood between them.
Vladimir Horngaard: A petition at the stones has not been called since the first winter of our order. State your business, boy, before the wind takes my patience.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: You have the right of it, lad. Speak plainly. We have no chairs out here, and the commander's knees were bad even when he had blood in them.
Cosmin [afraid]: I ask the table to hear the matter of Ioan, your last squire, whom your book names a coward. I have his blood, and I say the book lies.
Vladimir Horngaard [weary]: It is my hand in that book, cooper's son. Sit in the snow, if you must, and we will hear what you have brought to prove me a liar.
-> hearing_case

~ hearing_case
Narrator: A heavy ledger rests on Vladimir's armoured knee, its vellum stiff with frost. He does not need to look down to read the line.
Vladimir Horngaard: Ioan, son of the mason. Left his post on the night the hall fell. One word stands beside his name, and I wrote it when the roof was still smoking.
Vladimir Horngaard: Who speaks for a boy four hundred years dead, and what have you brought into the cold besides his descendant's stubbornness?
-> hearing_menu

~ hearing_menu
* [if flag.ioan_stone_read and not flag.hear_stone] (Read the words cut on the back of the last headstone.) -> hear_stone
* [if flag.vladimir_last_words_known and not flag.hear_words] (Give the dragon's last words: let them go home.) -> hear_words
* [if not flag.hear_godfrey] (Ask Sir Godfrey Gwilym what he did.) -> hear_godfrey
* [if not flag.hear_history] [History DC 13] (Argue from the Order's own rolls.) -> hear_history_ok | hear_history_miss
* [if not flag.hear_persuade] [Persuasion DC 14] (Speak for Ioan.) -> hear_persuade_ok | hear_persuade_miss
* (That is all we have.) -> hearing_vote

~ hear_stone
set hear_stone
set hearing_votes += 1
Narrator: You brush the rime from the back of the last marker and read the cramped, hurried letters chipped into the rough slate.
Vladimir Horngaard: He cut every stone while the vanguard was breaking at the gate. His chisel work was always crooked when he was in a hurry.
Cosmin: He stayed until the others had their names over them. A boy running for his life does not stop to spell all his dead friends properly.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_words
set hear_words
set hearing_votes += 1
Narrator: You repeat the words that lingered in the ruin's cold air. Around the graves, five grey heads lift at once.
Vladimir Horngaard [sad]: Let them go home. He spoke that in the courtyard with his last breath, when the smoke was in our throats, and I would not hear it.
Vladimir Horngaard [weary]: A squire would not know our lord's last command unless a knight gave it to him to carry. And I set his name down in the ash for obeying.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [sad]: It was a word for the valley, and we would not carry it. We were very proud, then, and very loud, and only the boy listened.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_godfrey
set hear_godfrey
set hearing_votes += 1
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [sad]: That was my mare. The grey one with the white pastern. I put the reins in his hand myself at the postern gate, because his own pony was lame.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym: I meant to tell the clerk in the morning, Vladimir. Only by morning there was no clerk, and the house was falling, and it went from me.
Vladimir Horngaard [weary]: Four centuries at the same table, Godfrey, and you might have mentioned the horse before the boy's kin came freezing to our door.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_history_ok
set hear_history
set hearing_votes += 1
Narrator: You cite the Order's own rolls: no brother or squire is marked fled unless two witnesses saw his back on the road.
Vladimir Horngaard: You have read our law better than our clerk did. Nobody saw him go, because everyone who looked toward the road that night was already dead.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_history_miss
set hear_history
Narrator: You reach for a precedent in the old rolls, but the names and the winters tangle in the telling, and the knights only stare at you.
Vladimir Horngaard: That was a rule of the Order's third chapter, stranger, and it was struck before the hall fell. Keep to what you know.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_persuade_ok
set hear_persuade
set hearing_votes += 1
Narrator: You speak quietly of the stone-cutter's boy who rode into the dark alone, and the cooper's boy who has slept many nights on the frost to clear him.
Cosmin: I do not care if I freeze, my lord. Only don't let my family go on being the ones who left their brothers in the fire.
Vladimir Horngaard [sad]: We have kept our anger warm a long time, and it has done nothing for the dead. Perhaps it is time it did some small thing for the living.
-> hearing_menu

~ hear_persuade_miss
set hear_persuade
Narrator: Your plea runs on into the wind, and the dead knights look away toward the dark trees, where speeches have never mended anything.
Vladimir Horngaard [weary]: Spare us the sermon. We know what honour costs, stranger. We are sitting in it.
-> hearing_menu

~ hearing_vote
Narrator: Hooves drum without sound across the crusted snow, and a sixth phantom reins in hard beside the graves, his cloak still half-unclasped.
Sir Ottokar the Late [weary]: Did I miss it?
if flag.hearing_votes >= 3
-> hearing_won
endif
-> hearing_lost

~ hearing_won
quest the_squire done
Narrator: One by one, five grey gauntlets rise from the snow and lay themselves flat upon the empty air where the table would be.
Vladimir Horngaard: Five voices for the squire. Let the word be struck from the roll. Ioan of the stones kept his post, and rode by order of this house.
Narrator: Vladimir rises, the frost cracking from his greaves, and looks down at the shivering boy.
Cosmin [afraid]: Does that mean I may go home and tell my mother, my lord?
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [smile]: You may go home and fetch a thicker coat, Squire Cosmin. We have no beds left, and you are no good to the Order with your toes black.
Cosmin [smile]: Yes, Sir Godfrey. I have a coat at the inn. It has three buttons left, if the rats haven't had them.
Narrator: With a dry quill that leaves a faint grey crease in the vellum, Sir Godfrey draws a single straight line through the old word.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [smile]: Ioan: rode with my lord's last word, on my horse, on my order. And under it, Cosmin: squire. Go on, lad, before I forget how to spell Vallaki.
-> squire_gift

~ hearing_lost
Vladimir Horngaard: A grave and a story are not enough to unwrite the book of this house. The roll stands as it was written.
Sir Godfrey Gwilym [sad]: Keep your coat close tonight, lad. My memory is a sieve, and it may be that more comes back to me before the dawn.
Cosmin: I can stand the vigil instead, my lord. If you will not hear his name cleared, you can see how his blood holds a sword.
-> cosmin
